\subsection{Oracle Control Design}
\label{sec:oracle-design}

To isolate whether the bottleneck lies in extraction or aggregation, we introduce an oracle control. Gold FOL annotations (FOLIO) or DSL rules (ProofWriter) replace self-extracted constraints; Z3 evaluates them against each candidate answer, with scoring unchanged (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}). If the oracle produces large gains while self-extraction does not, the failure localizes to extraction quality. Results appear in \S\ref{sec:oracle}.

\subsection{Scope}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. We test twelve remediation strategies: nine prompt-level spanning six dimensions and three method-level (ICG, Contrastive, Debate) targeting the independence bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). Methods using trained extractors, external provers, or multi-model ensembles fall outside scope.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Self-Consistency and extensions.}
SC selects the most frequent answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}. Extensions include universal SC for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting with step-aware verification \citep{li2023diverse}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, kim2026reasc}. \citet{loo2025reevaluating} show that SC gains plateau early. Our Condorcet analysis explains this: $\kappa = 0.21$--$0.82$ compresses $K_{\text{eff}}$ to 1.20--3.62 for $K = 12$ (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}).

\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
Logic-LM \citep{pan2023logiclm} and LINC \citep{olausson2023linc} translate problems into FOL for external provers; LINC documents ${\sim}$40\% translation errors. More recent systems extend this: DyLoS \citep{xu2026dylos} selects solver types, Aristotle \citep{li2025aristotle} applies logic-complete decomposition, FoVer \citep{wang2025fover} trains FOL verifiers, and LogicAgent \citep{luo2025logicagent} employs multi-perspective grounding. Each introduces capabilities beyond the reasoning model. \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show that LLM formalization degrades with complexity, consistent with our findings. Our oracle controls confirm the aggregation mechanism is sound ($+14.22$\,pp FOLIO, $+11.50$\,pp ProofWriter); the failure localizes to self-extraction quality (\S\ref{sec:oracle}).

\paragraph{Test-time compute and verification.}
Scaling test-time computation through additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling} faces the same correlation challenge: same-model attempts collapse into correlated solutions. Reasoning models intensify this (QwQ-32B: $C_0 = 2.6\%$). Trained verifiers offer an alternative: outcome reward models \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}, process reward models \citep{lightman2024prm}, and Math-Shepherd \citep{wang2024mathshepherd}. Multi-agent debate refines answers through discussion \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}. Self-correction without external feedback degrades performance \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification}, paralleling our finding.

\paragraph{CJT and ensemble diversity.}
CJT \citep{condorcet1785} establishes that independent majority voting converges to truth. \citet{ladha1992correlated} prove correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size. \citet{lefort2024condorcet_llm} apply CJT to LLM ensemble classification. We extend this to reasoning-trace aggregation, measuring $\kappa$ and $K_{\text{eff}}$ across 15 conditions (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}).

